  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\"><em>The Curriculum of Horror</em>, published by Peter Lang Verlag in 2019, explores the intersection of horror, art, and pedagogy within curriculum design. The work engages with Quality Education frameworks and has secured presence in library collections and educational systems. To date, formal citation metrics remain nascent, reflecting the early trajectory typical of recent scholarly books in humanities publishing.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">James V. Grant's monograph addresses curriculum theory through the lens of horror\u2014a distinctive intervention that bridges film studies, pedagogy, and art education. The conceptual scope encompasses curriculum design, artistic practice, psychological dimensions of horror, and sociological implications for teaching and learning. This thematic architecture positions the work within contemporary conversations about how unconventional or provocative content can be pedagogically and ethically integrated into educational contexts.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">The book's relevance to Quality Education (UN Sustainable Development Goal 4) is documented in the scholarly record, reflecting alignment with global policy discourse around educational innovation and curriculum development. Library holdings data shows adoption in educational institutions catalogued through WorldCat, with subject classifications spanning both curriculum studies and film pedagogy\u2014indicating institutional recognition of its dual disciplinary contribution.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Publication through Peter Lang Verlag, an established academic press specializing in education and humanities scholarship, situates this monograph within a peer-reviewed publishing ecosystem. The work's DOI registration and indexing in OpenAIRE repositories confirm formal scholarly archiving, ensuring persistent discoverability and access infrastructure typical of contemporary academic books.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">While formal citation evidence is not yet apparent in the current record, the work remains early in its publication lifecycle. Library adoption and subject cataloguing suggest that classroom adoption and disciplinary awareness are building through institutional channels\u2014a pathway particularly characteristic of humanities monographs, where impact often accumulates through teaching integration and longer-term scholarly dialogue rather than rapid citation velocity.</p>",
